ML_PAGE("l3-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 3 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Chapter 3 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l3\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"l3n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">55 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. The hidden problem of investing is not lack of information\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s1\" title=\"1. The hidden problem of investing is not lack of information\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Too much information, not too little</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Online investing: convenience removes friction, including useful friction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s2\" title=\"2. Online investing: convenience removes friction, including useful friction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Online investing and friction</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Descriptive vs analytical information\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s3\" title=\"3. Descriptive vs analytical information\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Descriptive vs analytical information</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Example: descriptive vs analytical\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s4\" title=\"4. Example: descriptive vs analytical\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Example: descriptive vs analytical</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Information hierarchy: not all sources deserve equal trust\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s5\" title=\"5. Information hierarchy: not all sources deserve equal trust\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Information hierarchy</button></li><li data-full=\"6. The analytical skill: separate evidence from narrative\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s6\" title=\"6. The analytical skill: separate evidence from narrative\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Evidence vs narrative</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Regulation FD: information equality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s7\" title=\"7. Regulation FD: information equality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Regulation FD</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Shareholder reports and company filings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s8\" title=\"8. Shareholder reports and company filings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Shareholder reports and company filings</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Beware information designed to persuade you\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s9\" title=\"9. Beware information designed to persuade you\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Information designed to persuade</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Five defensive questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s10\" title=\"10. Five defensive questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Five defensive questions</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Market averages and indexes: what problem do they solve?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s11\" title=\"11. Market averages and indexes: what problem do they solve?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>What averages and indexes are for</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Why an index exists\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s12\" title=\"12. Why an index exists\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Why an index exists</button></li><li data-full=\"13. DJIA vs S&amp;P 500\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s13\" title=\"13. DJIA vs S&amp;P 500\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>DJIA vs S&amp;P 500</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Why index composition matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s14\" title=\"14. Why index composition matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Why index composition matters</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Equal weighting vs market weighting\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s15\" title=\"15. Equal weighting vs market weighting\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Equal weighting vs market weighting</button></li><li data-full=\"16. The chart on slide 25: two broad indexes can move similarly but not identically\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s16\" title=\"16. The chart on slide 25: two broad indexes can move similarly but not identically\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>DJIA vs S&amp;P 500 chart</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Bond market indicators\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s17\" title=\"17. Bond market indicators\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Bond market indicators</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Stockbrokers: intermediary, not magician\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s18\" title=\"18. Stockbrokers: intermediary, not magician\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Stockbrokers: intermediaries</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Street name\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s19\" title=\"19. Street name\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Street name</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Full-service vs discount brokerage\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s20\" title=\"20. Full-service vs discount brokerage\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Full-service vs discount brokerage</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Selecting a broker: incentive alignment matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s21\" title=\"21. Selecting a broker: incentive alignment matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Selecting a broker: incentives</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Types of brokerage accounts\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s22\" title=\"22. Types of brokerage accounts\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Types of brokerage accounts</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Order types: this is one of the most testable parts\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s23\" title=\"23. Order types: this is one of the most testable parts\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Order types</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Market order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s24\" title=\"24. Market order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Market order</button></li><li data-full=\"25. Limit order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s25\" title=\"25. Limit order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Limit order</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Market vs limit order - the key comparison\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s26\" title=\"26. Market vs limit order - the key comparison\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Market vs limit: key comparison</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Example\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s27\" title=\"27. Example\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Example: buying around RM10</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Fill-or-kill, day and GTC orders\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s28\" title=\"28. Fill-or-kill, day and GTC orders\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Fill-or-kill, day and GTC orders</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Stop-loss order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s29\" title=\"29. Stop-loss order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Stop-loss order</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Stop-limit order\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s30\" title=\"30. Stop-limit order\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Stop-limit order</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Buy stop for short sellers\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s31\" title=\"31. Buy stop for short sellers\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Buy stop for short sellers</button></li><li data-full=\"32. Day trading: activity is not the same as skill\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s32\" title=\"32. Day trading: activity is not the same as skill\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>Day trading</button></li><li data-full=\"33. Trading costs create a hurdle rate\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s33\" title=\"33. Trading costs create a hurdle rate\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Trading costs as a hurdle</button></li><li data-full=\"34. Investor protection: what protection actually protects\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s34\" title=\"34. Investor protection: what protection actually protects\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>What investor protection covers</button></li><li data-full=\"35. Mediation vs arbitration\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s35\" title=\"35. Mediation vs arbitration\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Mediation vs arbitration</button></li><li data-full=\"36. Investment advisors: registration does not guarantee quality\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s36\" title=\"36. Investment advisors: registration does not guarantee quality\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Investment advisors</button></li><li data-full=\"37. Good advisors should ask you questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s37\" title=\"37. Good advisors should ask you questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Good advisors should ask you questions</button></li><li data-full=\"38. Investment clubs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s38\" title=\"38. Investment clubs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Investment clubs</button></li><li data-full=\"39. The hidden architecture of Chapter 3\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s39\" title=\"39. The hidden architecture of Chapter 3\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>The four-layer chapter map</button></li><li data-full=\"40. Apply: stock recommendation from social media\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s40\" title=\"40. Apply: stock recommendation from social media\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Apply: a social-media stock tip</button></li><li data-full=\"41. Apply: choosing an order type\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s41\" title=\"41. Apply: choosing an order type\"><span class=\"toc-no\">41</span>Apply: choosing an order type</button></li><li data-full=\"42. Analyse: why market order and limit order can both be rational\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s42\" title=\"42. Analyse: why market order and limit order can both be rational\"><span class=\"toc-no\">42</span>Analyse: market vs limit, both rational</button></li><li data-full=\"43. Evaluate: &quot;An index increased, therefore my portfolio should also have increased&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s43\" title=\"43. Evaluate: &quot;An index increased, therefore my portfolio should also have increased&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">43</span>Evaluate: the index rose, so should I</button></li><li data-full=\"44. Evaluate: &quot;Online trading is better because it is cheaper and faster&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s44\" title=\"44. Evaluate: &quot;Online trading is better because it is cheaper and faster&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">44</span>Evaluate: online trading is better</button></li><li data-full=\"45. Evaluate: &quot;Stop-loss orders guarantee maximum loss&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s45\" title=\"45. Evaluate: &quot;Stop-loss orders guarantee maximum loss&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">45</span>Evaluate: stop-losses cap the loss</button></li><li data-full=\"46. Evaluate: &quot;A professional advisor removes investment risk&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s46\" title=\"46. Evaluate: &quot;A professional advisor removes investment risk&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">46</span>Evaluate: advisors remove risk</button></li><li data-full=\"47. Bloom - Remember\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s47\" title=\"47. Bloom - Remember\"><span class=\"toc-no\">47</span>Bloom - Remember</button></li><li data-full=\"48. Bloom - Understand\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s48\" title=\"48. Bloom - Understand\"><span class=\"toc-no\">48</span>Bloom - Understand</button></li><li data-full=\"49. Bloom - Apply\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s49\" title=\"49. Bloom - Apply\"><span class=\"toc-no\">49</span>Bloom - Apply</button></li><li data-full=\"50. Bloom - Analyse\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s50\" title=\"50. Bloom - Analyse\"><span class=\"toc-no\">50</span>Bloom - Analyse</button></li><li data-full=\"51. Bloom - Evaluate\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s51\" title=\"51. Bloom - Evaluate\"><span class=\"toc-no\">51</span>Bloom - Evaluate</button></li><li data-full=\"52. Bloom - Create\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s52\" title=\"52. Bloom - Create\"><span class=\"toc-no\">52</span>Bloom - Create</button></li><li data-full=\"53. What I would expect in an exam\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s53\" title=\"53. What I would expect in an exam\"><span class=\"toc-no\">53</span>Likely exam questions</button></li><li data-full=\"54. The six ideas I would master hardest\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s54\" title=\"54. The six ideas I would master hardest\"><span class=\"toc-no\">54</span>Six ideas to master</button></li><li data-full=\"55. One final integrated case\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3n-s55\" title=\"55. One final integrated case\"><span class=\"toc-no\">55</span>Integrated case</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 3 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked numbers, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>55 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>Chapter 3 is less about \"what is a stock?\" and more about <strong>how an investor turns raw information into an actual trading decision</strong>.</p>\n<p>If Chapter 1 was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{What should I invest in?}$$</div>\n<p>and Chapter 2 was:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{How do securities markets work?}$$</div>\n<p>then Chapter 3 is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{How do I obtain information, interpret the market, choose an intermediary, and execute the trade correctly?}}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter formally covers online investing, investment information, market averages and indexes, brokers, brokerage accounts, order types, transaction costs, investor protection, advisors and investment clubs.</p>\n<p>The whole chapter can be compressed into this chain:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Information → Analysis → Benchmark → Broker/Account → Order → Execution → Monitoring\"><span>Information</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analysis</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Benchmark</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Broker/Account</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Order</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Execution</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>That is the structure I would keep in your head.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Chapter 3 - Investment Information and Securities Transactions</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. The hidden problem of investing is not lack of information</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Today, investors can access:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>stock screeners,</li>\n<li>current quotations,</li>\n<li>charts,</li>\n<li>financial reports,</li>\n<li>market news,</li>\n<li>company filings,</li>\n<li>analyst commentary.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The chapter explicitly presents online tools for financial planning, screening stocks, tracking performance and monitoring portfolio value.</p>\n<p>So the modern problem is not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I cannot find information.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is increasingly:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>\"Which information matters, which source is trustworthy, and how should I interpret it?\"</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a much harder problem.</p>\n<p>More information does not automatically create better decisions.</p>\n<p>Sometimes it creates:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More noise}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More confidence}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More trading}$$</div>\n<p>without actually creating:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More knowledge}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Online investing: convenience removes friction, including useful friction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter points out that the Internet makes research and execution much easier, but warns that investors lose the \"safety net\" of a live broker, should be skeptical of free advice, should understand what they are buying, and should beware frequent trading and margin risk.</p>\n<p>The obvious advantage is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Speed + access + lower barriers}}$$</div>\n<p>But there is a behavioral downside.</p>\n<p>Imagine the old process:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Research → call broker → discuss → place trade\"><span>Research</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>call broker</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>discuss</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>place trade</span></div>\n<p>There was friction.</p>\n<p>Today:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{See TikTok}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{tap Buy}$$</div>\n<p>That sounds efficient.</p>\n<p>It can also be catastrophically stupid.</p>\n<p>The disappearance of friction is beneficial only when the investor's decision quality is already high.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Descriptive vs analytical information</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is one of the chapter's most important distinctions.</p>\n<p>The slides define:</p>\n<h4>Descriptive information</h4>\n<p>Factual information on past behavior of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the economy,</li>\n<li>the market,</li>\n<li>an industry,</li>\n<li>a company,</li>\n<li>an investment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Analytical information</h4>\n<p>Current information combined with projections and recommendations about potential investments.</p>\n<p>The easy way to think about this is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Descriptive = What happened?}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Analytical = What might happen, and what does it mean?}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Example: descriptive vs analytical</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose Company A reports:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Revenue grew 15\\%}$$</div>\n<p>That is descriptive.</p>\n<p>It tells you what happened.</p>\n<p>Now suppose an analyst says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Revenue growth is expected to slow to 5% because demand is weakening.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is analytical.</p>\n<p>The second statement contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>interpretation;</li>\n<li>assumptions;</li>\n<li>forecasting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This distinction matters because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Facts are not the same thing as conclusions}}$$</div>\n<p>An investor should constantly ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What part of this statement is observed data, and what part is judgment?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That question alone will save you from a lot of bad analysis.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Information hierarchy: not all sources deserve equal trust</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l3_info_layers\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>The further a source is from audited, primary evidence, the more skeptical you should be.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter lists multiple sources of economic, company and investment information, including newspapers, financial journals, government publications, business periodicals and institutional news services.</p>\n<p>For company-specific information, it highlights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>company websites;</li>\n<li>shareholder reports;</li>\n<li>regulatory filings;</li>\n<li>brokerage reports;</li>\n<li>subscription services;</li>\n<li>investment letters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But you should not treat all of them as equally authoritative.</p>\n<p>Think in layers.</p>\n<h4>Layer 1 - Primary source</h4>\n<p>Company filing, regulatory disclosure, audited statements.</p>\n<h4>Layer 2 - Professional interpretation</h4>\n<p>Broker research, financial news, analyst reports.</p>\n<h4>Layer 3 - Opinion</h4>\n<p>Blogs, forums, newsletters.</p>\n<h4>Layer 4 - Anonymous commentary</h4>\n<p>Random social media post by someone whose profile picture is a Lamborghini.</p>\n<p>The lower you go, the more skepticism you should apply.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. The analytical skill: separate evidence from narrative</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you read:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Company X is expanding aggressively into AI and will dominate the sector.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>What is the evidence?</p>\n<p>You should look for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>actual revenue;</li>\n<li>margins;</li>\n<li>capital expenditure;</li>\n<li>customer growth;</li>\n<li>competition;</li>\n<li>financing;</li>\n<li>regulatory filings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A sophisticated investor asks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{What evidence supports the story?}}$$</div>\n<p>rather than:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Does the story sound exciting?}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Regulation FD: information equality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter notes that Regulation FD requires critical company information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public.</p>\n<p>The underlying principle is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{material information should not be selectively disclosed}}$$</div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because markets work better when access to important information is not reserved for a privileged circle.</p>\n<p>This connects directly to Chapter 2's discussion of insider information.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Shareholder reports and company filings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter mentions stockholder reports and annual regulatory filings as important information sources.</p>\n<p>Conceptually, these are important because they help you analyze:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Profitability}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Financial position}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cash flow}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risks}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Management discussion}$$</div>\n<p>The figures on slides 46-47 show examples of stockholder-report pages presenting company performance visually.</p>\n<p>The point is not to admire the graphics.</p>\n<p>It is to ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which numbers actually reflect the economics of the business?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Beware information designed to persuade you</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter specifically warns against:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>false news;</li>\n<li>overly optimistic opinions;</li>\n<li>pump-and-dump schemes;</li>\n<li>get-rich-quick promotions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A pump-and-dump works roughly like this:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">  Promoter buys cheap stock\n      │\n      ▼\n  Promoter hypes stock\n      │\n      ▼\n  Demand rises\n      │\n      ▼\n  Price rises\n      │\n      ▼\n  Promoter sells\n      │\n      ▼\n  Late buyers are left holding garbage\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is a beautiful example of why <strong>source incentives matter</strong>.</p>\n<p>Always ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{What does the person giving me this information gain if I believe them?}}$$</div>\n<p>That is a higher-level investment question.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Five defensive questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter recommends asking:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Is the seller licensed?</li>\n<li>Is the investment registered?</li>\n<li>How does risk compare with potential reward?</li>\n<li>Do I understand the investment?</li>\n<li>Where can I seek help?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These can be compressed into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Legitimacy}\n+\n\\text{Risk}\n+\n\\text{Understanding}\n+\n\\text{Recourse}\n}$$</div>\n<p>If you cannot explain how the investment works, how it makes money and how it can lose money, that is already a red flag.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Market averages and indexes: what problem do they solve?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives three main uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>gauge market conditions;</li>\n<li>compare portfolio performance against a broad market;</li>\n<li>study market cycles and trends.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is fundamentally a <strong>benchmarking problem</strong>.</p>\n<p>Suppose your portfolio earned:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Was that good?</p>\n<p>Impossible to answer in isolation.</p>\n<p>If the market returned:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$-10\\%$$</div>\n<p>then 8% looks excellent.</p>\n<p>If the market returned:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$25\\%$$</div>\n<p>then 8% looks rather poor.</p>\n<p>So performance must be evaluated relative to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{a relevant benchmark}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Why an index exists</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Imagine tracking 500 stocks individually every day.</p>\n<p>Not very practical.</p>\n<p>An index compresses the market into a single measure.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Index = summary statistic for a group of securities}}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter distinguishes averages from indexes by noting that averages reflect arithmetic price behavior while indexes measure current values relative to a base period.</p>\n<p>The deeper point is that both are trying to answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What is happening to the market represented by this basket?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. DJIA vs S&amp;P 500</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes the Dow Jones Industrial Average as containing 30 major stocks and the S&amp;P 500 as containing 500 companies across major industries, making it broader and more representative.</p>\n<p>Do not memorize only:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Dow = 30</p>\n<p>S&amp;P = 500</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Ask instead:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Which one represents the broader market better?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The chapter explicitly answers:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{S\\&amp;P 500}}$$</div>\n<p>because it contains many more companies across industries.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Why index composition matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose one index contains mostly large companies.</p>\n<p>Another contains mainly small companies.</p>\n<p>If they perform differently, that does not necessarily mean one index is \"wrong.\"</p>\n<p>They are measuring different market segments.</p>\n<p>The chapter gives examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Russell 1000 = large companies;</li>\n<li>Russell 2000 = small companies;</li>\n<li>EAFE MSCI = foreign markets;</li>\n<li>Nasdaq Composite = Nasdaq-listed stocks, often used as a technology-oriented indicator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Benchmark selection must match the exposure being evaluated}}$$</div>\n<p>Comparing a Malaysian small-cap portfolio against the S&amp;P 500 is not automatically meaningful.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Equal weighting vs market weighting</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_index\"><div class=\"vis-title\">How index construction changes the answer</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Move one stock&#x27;s price and compare a price-weighted, value-weighted and equal-weighted index.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter notes that the Value Line Composite Index uses equal weighting to avoid large companies dominating the index.</p>\n<p>This reveals something important:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>An index is not neutral. Its construction methodology affects what it measures.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Suppose you have:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Company A: huge;</li>\n<li>Company B: medium;</li>\n<li>Company C: tiny.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a market-value-weighted index, Company A may dominate.</p>\n<p>In an equal-weighted index:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$A=B=C$$</div>\n<p>in weight.</p>\n<p>Therefore the same stock universe can produce different index behavior depending on construction method.</p>\n<p>That is a proper analytical insight.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. The chart on slide 25: two broad indexes can move similarly but not identically</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chart comparing the DJIA with the S&amp;P 500 from 2010-2012 shows that both broadly followed similar market movements, but with different magnitudes at various points.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because they do not contain identical securities or identical weighting rules.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{same market environment} \\neq \\text{identical index return}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Bond market indicators</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines bond yield as total return associated with buying a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity, involving interest plus/minus changes in bond value.</p>\n<p>The key idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Coupon is not the same thing as yield.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you buy a bond below or above face value, the overall return can differ from the coupon rate.</p>\n<p>Later chapters will formalize this.</p>\n<p>For now, remember:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Bond yield reflects return relative to price paid}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Stockbrokers: intermediary, not magician</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says stockbrokers act as intermediaries between buyers and sellers and execute client orders.</p>\n<p>That means a broker's core function is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{execution}}$$</div>\n<p>not guaranteeing profits.</p>\n<p>The brokerage may also provide:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>research;</li>\n<li>account statements;</li>\n<li>administrative services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But an investor should not confuse:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Access to advice}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{certainty of outcome}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Street name</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter explains that securities may be registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the client who is the beneficial owner.</p>\n<p>Think of it as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Legal registration convenience}\n\\neq\n\\text{economic ownership}$$</div>\n<p>The broker holds the securities administratively.</p>\n<p>The investor still owns the economic interest.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Full-service vs discount brokerage</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter distinguishes:</p>\n<h4>Full-service brokers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>broad services;</li>\n<li>research;</li>\n<li>advice;</li>\n<li>higher fees.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Premium discount brokers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>lower commissions;</li>\n<li>limited research/advice.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Basic discount brokers</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>mainly execution;</li>\n<li>little or no advice;</li>\n<li>deep-discount commissions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The higher-level question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What are you paying for?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>If you need:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{advice + planning + research}$$</div>\n<p>then higher fees might have some justification.</p>\n<p>If you already know exactly what you are buying and only need execution, paying for unnecessary advisory services makes less sense.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Selecting a broker: incentive alignment matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter warns about <strong>churning</strong>, where excessive trading may be encouraged to generate commissions.</p>\n<p>This is another incentive problem.</p>\n<p>If broker revenue is tied to transaction volume:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More trades}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{More commissions}$$</div>\n<p>But more trades are not necessarily better for the client.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Broker incentive} \\neq \\text{Investor objective}}$$</div>\n<p>unless properly aligned.</p>\n<p>That is the economic logic behind the warning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Types of brokerage accounts</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes four account types:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>custodial;</li>\n<li>cash;</li>\n<li>margin;</li>\n<li>wrap.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The distinctions are easiest to remember by asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Who controls the account and where does the money come from?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h4>Cash account</h4>\n<p>You fund the transactions yourself.</p>\n<h4>Margin account</h4>\n<p>Broker extends borrowing privileges.</p>\n<h4>Custodial account</h4>\n<p>Adult manages on behalf of a minor.</p>\n<h4>Wrap account</h4>\n<p>Professional money manager makes investment decisions for an annual fee.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Order types: this is one of the most testable parts</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l3_order_matrix\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>No order gives certainty of both execution and price: each order type picks a side of the trade-off.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Chapter 3 becomes very practical here.</p>\n<p>A trade order has two possible objectives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Execution certainty}}$$</div>\n<p>and</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Price control}}$$</div>\n<p>Usually you cannot maximize both perfectly.</p>\n<p>This is the logic behind market orders and limit orders.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Market order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines a market order as buying or selling at the best available price when the order is placed. It is the fastest way to obtain execution.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Market order prioritizes execution}}$$</div>\n<p>You are essentially saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Get me into/out of the position now.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But you do <strong>not</strong> control the exact price.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. Limit order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines a limit order as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>buy at or below a specified price;</li>\n<li>sell at or above a specified price.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If the specified price is not reached, the order does not execute.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Limit order prioritizes price}}$$</div>\n<p>You are saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Only trade if I can get this price or better.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The trade-off:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{price protection}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{execution uncertainty}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Market vs limit order - the key comparison</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Market Order</th>\n<th>Limit Order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Priority</td>\n<td>Execution</td>\n<td>Price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fill likely?</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Not guaranteed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exact price controlled?</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Poor execution price</td>\n<td>No execution</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That table is worth remembering.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Example</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A stock currently trades around RM10.</p>\n<p>You want to buy it.</p>\n<h4>Market order</h4>\n<p>You may receive:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10.02$$</div>\n<p>or:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10.10$$</div>\n<p>depending on liquidity and market movement.</p>\n<h4>Limit order at RM9.80</h4>\n<p>The order only executes at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}9.80 \\text{ or lower}$$</div>\n<p>If the stock never falls to RM9.80:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{No trade}}$$</div>\n<p>So the question is not:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which order is better?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Which risk do I care about more: execution risk or price risk?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Fill-or-kill, day and GTC orders</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes:</p>\n<h4>Fill-or-kill</h4>\n<p>Execute immediately or cancel.</p>\n<h4>Day order</h4>\n<p>Expires at end of trading day if unfilled.</p>\n<h4>Good-'til-canceled</h4>\n<p>Remains active for an extended period unless executed or canceled.</p>\n<p>The deeper idea is that every order has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{price conditions} + \\text{time conditions}}$$</div>\n<p>Limit tells you the price constraint.</p>\n<p>Day/GTC/FOK tell you the time constraint.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Stop-loss order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says a stop-loss order is placed at a trigger price and, once activated, becomes a market order.</p>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$P=\\text{RM}20$$</div>\n<p>You set stop at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}17$$</div>\n<p>If price reaches RM17, the stop triggers and becomes a market sell.</p>\n<p>This is often used to limit downside.</p>\n<p>But here's the important subtlety:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Stop price} \\neq \\text{guaranteed execution price}}$$</div>\n<p>If the stock gaps from RM18 to RM14, you might execute around RM14 rather than RM17.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Stop-limit order</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A stop-limit order adds price control.</p>\n<p>When the stop activates, it becomes a limit order instead of a market order. The chapter notes that this can prevent sale at an undesirable price, but the trade may fail to execute if prices keep falling.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<h4>Stop order</h4>\n<p>Higher chance of exiting.</p>\n<p>But poor-price risk.</p>\n<h4>Stop-limit</h4>\n<p>Better price protection.</p>\n<p>But greater non-execution risk.</p>\n<p>Again:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{execution certainty vs price certainty}}$$</div>\n<p>This principle keeps appearing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Buy stop for short sellers</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter notes that stop orders can also be used to buy stock in order to limit risk on a short position.</p>\n<p>Suppose you short at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>You fear the price rising.</p>\n<p>Set a buy stop at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}60$$</div>\n<p>If price hits RM60, a buy order is activated to close the short.</p>\n<p>So for:</p>\n<h4>Long position</h4>\n<p>Protective stop typically = sell stop below current price.</p>\n<h4>Short position</h4>\n<p>Protective stop typically = buy stop above current price.</p>\n<p>That is worth understanding rather than memorizing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. Day trading: activity is not the same as skill</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines day trading as rapidly buying and selling securities during the day to seek quick profits, and describes it as risky, particularly with margin, while frequent trading increases commissions.</p>\n<p>The conceptual trap is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More decisions}$$</div>\n<p>feels like:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More control}$$</div>\n<p>But in reality:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{More trades}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{more transaction costs}\n+\n\\text{more opportunities for error}$$</div>\n<p>The chapter's earlier warning about frequent trading follows the same logic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. Trading costs create a hurdle rate</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you buy an investment for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div>\n<p>You incur RM50 total costs entering and exiting.</p>\n<p>Before making any real profit, the investment must first earn enough to recover:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}50$$</div>\n<p>So transaction costs create a hurdle:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Gross return} - \\text{costs} = \\text{net return}}$$</div>\n<p>A strategy that appears profitable before costs may be unattractive afterward.</p>\n<p>This is especially important for high-frequency strategies.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. Investor protection: what protection actually protects</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter discusses SIPC protection against broker financial failure but explicitly says that it does not guarantee investors against bad advice or churning.</p>\n<p>That distinction is essential.</p>\n<p>Investor protection usually means protection against certain institutional failures or misconduct.</p>\n<p>It does <strong>not</strong> mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Your investments cannot lose value}}$$</div>\n<p>A bad investment decision remains your risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. Mediation vs arbitration</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter distinguishes:</p>\n<h4>Mediation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>informal;</li>\n<li>voluntary;</li>\n<li>nonbinding if no agreement.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Arbitration</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>more formal;</li>\n<li>parties present arguments;</li>\n<li>binding arbitration requires acceptance of the panel's decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The easiest memory device:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Mediation = negotiate}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Arbitration = adjudicate}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">36. Investment advisors: registration does not guarantee quality</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says advisors should be registered but explicitly warns that registration does not guarantee competence. It recommends considering experience and professional designations.</p>\n<p>This is important because people often make this mistake:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{licensed}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{good}$$</div>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>Licensing mainly means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{met regulatory requirements}}$$</div>\n<p>It does not guarantee:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{judgment}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{skill}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{integrity}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{outperformance}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">37. Good advisors should ask you questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter says a good advisor should ask many questions to understand the investor's expertise.</p>\n<p>That aligns directly with Chapter 1.</p>\n<p>The advisor needs to understand:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{goals}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{horizon}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{risk tolerance}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{financial circumstances}$$</div>\n<p>before recommending a portfolio.</p>\n<p>If an advisor immediately recommends a product without understanding the investor, something is backwards.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">38. Investment clubs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter describes investment clubs as legal partnerships where investors pool money and knowledge, make recommendations and analyze stocks together.</p>\n<p>Potential benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>shared research;</li>\n<li>education;</li>\n<li>pooled resources.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Potential weakness:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{group confidence can exceed group competence}}$$</div>\n<p>Ten people agreeing enthusiastically can still all be wrong.</p>\n<p>So collective decision-making helps only if the process remains analytical.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">39. The hidden architecture of Chapter 3</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Almost the entire chapter can be organized into four layers.</p>\n<h4>Layer 1 - Information acquisition</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{news}\n+\n\\text{company reports}\n+\n\\text{filings}\n+\n\\text{data}$$</div>\n<h4>Layer 2 - Information interpretation</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{descriptive}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{analytical}$$</div>\n<h4>Layer 3 - Market context</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{indexes}\n+\n\\text{benchmarks}\n+\n\\text{yield indicators}$$</div>\n<h4>Layer 4 - Execution</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{broker}\n+\n\\text{account}\n+\n\\text{order}\n+\n\\text{transaction cost}$$</div>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Good investing}\n=\n\\text{good information}\n+\n\\text{good interpretation}\n+\n\\text{good execution}\n}$$</div>\n<p>A failure in any one of these can destroy the result.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">40. Apply: stock recommendation from social media</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose someone posts:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"ABC stock will double in three months. Institutional investors are secretly accumulating. Buy now before it's too late.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>How should you analyze it?</p>\n<h4>Step 1 - Identify the source</h4>\n<p>Who posted it?</p>\n<h4>Step 2 - Separate descriptive from analytical claims</h4>\n<p>\"Trading volume increased 50%\" = descriptive.</p>\n<p>\"Institutions are secretly accumulating\" = analytical/speculative unless supported.</p>\n<h4>Step 3 - Verify primary evidence</h4>\n<p>Company filing? Official announcement? Financial statements?</p>\n<h4>Step 4 - Analyze incentives</h4>\n<p>Does the promoter already own the stock?</p>\n<h4>Step 5 - Assess risk/reward</h4>\n<p>What happens if the claim is false?</p>\n<p>That is Chapter 3 applied properly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">41. Apply: choosing an order type</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a thinly traded stock is quoted around RM5.00.</p>\n<p>You want to buy, but you absolutely do not want to pay above:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}5.20$$</div>\n<p>Should you place a market order?</p>\n<p>Probably not.</p>\n<p>A market order prioritizes execution rather than price.</p>\n<p>A limit order at:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{RM}5.20$$</div>\n<p>is more consistent with your constraint.</p>\n<p>Because the instruction is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Buy only if } P\\le \\text{RM}5.20}$$</div>\n<p>That is an <strong>application</strong> question.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">42. Analyse: why market order and limit order can both be rational</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose Investor A must exit immediately because of an emergency.</p>\n<p>Investor B is trying to purchase a stock only if it becomes sufficiently cheap.</p>\n<p>Investor A values:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{execution certainty}$$</div>\n<p>Investor B values:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{price certainty}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>market order may fit A;</li>\n<li>limit order may fit B.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Same market.</p>\n<p>Different objective.</p>\n<p>This reinforces Chapter 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Suitability depends on the investor's objective}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">43. Evaluate: \"An index increased, therefore my portfolio should also have increased\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Wrong.</p>\n<p>An index represents a specific basket and weighting methodology.</p>\n<p>Your portfolio may differ in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>sector;</li>\n<li>company size;</li>\n<li>geography;</li>\n<li>asset type;</li>\n<li>weighting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore benchmark relevance matters.</p>\n<p>The chapter itself shows numerous indexes designed for different market segments.</p>\n<p>The proper question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Is the benchmark representative of my portfolio's investment universe?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">44. Evaluate: \"Online trading is better because it is cheaper and faster\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Incomplete.</p>\n<p>Online trading improves convenience and access to information, but the chapter warns about:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>lack of human guidance;</li>\n<li>unreliable advice;</li>\n<li>frequent trading;</li>\n<li>transaction costs;</li>\n<li>margin risks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Better execution technology}\n\\neq\n\\text{better investment decisions}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">45. Evaluate: \"Stop-loss orders guarantee maximum loss\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>False.</p>\n<p>A stop order becomes a market order once triggered.</p>\n<p>If the market gaps sharply downward, execution can occur below the stop level.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Stop price = trigger, not guaranteed sale price}}$$</div>\n<p>A stop-limit provides more price control but can fail to execute entirely.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">46. Evaluate: \"A professional advisor removes investment risk\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>No.</p>\n<p>The advisor may improve:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>planning;</li>\n<li>diversification;</li>\n<li>analysis;</li>\n<li>discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>But market risk remains.</p>\n<p>The chapter itself states that registration does not guarantee competence.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Advice can improve process, not eliminate uncertainty}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">47. Bloom - Remember</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>descriptive vs analytical information;</li>\n<li>index;</li>\n<li>broker;</li>\n<li>cash account;</li>\n<li>margin account;</li>\n<li>market order;</li>\n<li>limit order;</li>\n<li>stop order;</li>\n<li>stop-limit;</li>\n<li>mediation;</li>\n<li>arbitration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Necessary, but basic.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s48\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">48. Bloom - Understand</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You should explain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>why primary sources are generally more reliable;</li>\n<li>why indexes are used as benchmarks;</li>\n<li>why market and limit orders involve different trade-offs;</li>\n<li>why online investing can increase behavioral mistakes;</li>\n<li>why excessive trading can reduce returns.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">49. Bloom - Apply</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Given a situation, choose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>information source;</li>\n<li>benchmark;</li>\n<li>brokerage account;</li>\n<li>order type.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Investor wants to buy only if a stock falls below RM40.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Answer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Limit buy order at RM40 or lower}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">50. Bloom - Analyse</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Given two information sources:</p>\n<h4>Source A</h4>\n<p>Audited annual report.</p>\n<h4>Source B</h4>\n<p>Anonymous forum saying earnings will \"explode.\"</p>\n<p>You should analyze:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>reliability;</li>\n<li>incentives;</li>\n<li>verifiability;</li>\n<li>descriptive vs analytical content.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Not just say A is \"better.\"</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s51\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">51. Bloom - Evaluate</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a portfolio returned 12%.</p>\n<p>You should not immediately say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Excellent performance.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Instead evaluate:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_p$$</div>\n<p>relative to an appropriate benchmark.</p>\n<p>If benchmark return:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$18\\%$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$12\\%$$</div>\n<p>may represent underperformance.</p>\n<p>Performance must be contextual.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">52. Bloom - Create</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you are asked:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Design an investment decision process for a student who wants to invest in a technology company.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong process could be:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">  1. Read company filing\n      │\n      ▼\n  2. Review financial performance\n      │\n      ▼\n  3. Compare against industry competitors\n      │\n      ▼\n  4. Separate factual data from analyst forecasts\n      │\n      ▼\n  5. Compare performance with relevant technology benchmark\n      │\n      ▼\n  6. Define acceptable entry price\n      │\n      ▼\n  7. Choose market/limit order accordingly\n      │\n      ▼\n  8. Monitor position\n</code></pre>\n<p>That is genuine Bloom Level 6 reasoning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">53. What I would expect in an exam</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>I would prepare for questions such as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Differentiate descriptive and analytical investment information with examples.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate the advantages and risks of online investing.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain why investors use market indexes and benchmarks.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Compare the DJIA and S&amp;P 500.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain how benchmark selection affects performance evaluation.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Compare full-service and discount brokers.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Differentiate cash, margin and wrap accounts.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Compare market, limit, stop and stop-limit orders.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain why a stop-loss does not guarantee a maximum loss.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Discuss transaction costs and how excessive trading can affect investment performance.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate the role of investment advisors.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Explain how investors can protect themselves from online scams.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Those force you into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Apply → Analyse → Evaluate\"><span>Apply</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analyse</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Evaluate</span></div>\n<p>rather than mere recall.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">54. The six ideas I would master hardest</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If the exam were tomorrow, I would focus heavily on these:</p>\n<h4>1. Information quality</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Fact} \\neq \\text{forecast} \\neq \\text{opinion}}$$</div>\n<h4>2. Benchmarking</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Return is meaningless without context}}$$</div>\n<h4>3. Broker choice</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Pay for services you actually need}}$$</div>\n<h4>4. Market vs limit</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Execution certainty vs price certainty}}$$</div>\n<h4>5. Stop vs stop-limit</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Exit certainty vs price protection}}$$</div>\n<h4>6. Transaction discipline</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{More trading does not automatically mean more return}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3n-s55\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">55. One final integrated case</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose you see an online recommendation that says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Buy TechCo immediately. It will double.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You verify the company's annual report and find earnings are declining.</p>\n<p>The sector index is up 20%, while TechCo is down 15%.</p>\n<p>You still like the long-term business but only want to buy below RM25.</p>\n<p>The stock currently trades at RM28.</p>\n<p>How should Chapter 3 guide you?</p>\n<h4>Information</h4>\n<p>The online recommendation is analytical/opinion-based.</p>\n<p>The annual report provides more credible descriptive information.</p>\n<h4>Benchmark</h4>\n<p>TechCo underperformed its sector benchmark.</p>\n<p>That may indicate company-specific weakness.</p>\n<h4>Decision</h4>\n<p>You are willing to invest, but only below RM25.</p>\n<h4>Execution</h4>\n<p>A market order would violate your price condition.</p>\n<p>A limit order:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Buy at RM25 or lower}}$$</div>\n<p>better matches the objective.</p>\n<p>That one scenario combines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{source evaluation}\n+\n\\text{descriptive vs analytical information}\n+\n\\text{benchmarking}\n+\n\\text{order selection}$$</div>\n<p>which is basically the chapter working as a system.</p>\n<p>The chapter's formal learning goals ultimately converge on exactly this: using information intelligently, understanding market indicators, selecting suitable intermediaries and executing transactions properly.</p>\n<p>The deepest lesson is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Investment success does not end with choosing the right asset.}\n}$$</div>\n<p>You also need:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{reliable information}\n+\n\\text{correct interpretation}\n+\n\\text{appropriate benchmark}\n+\n\\text{proper execution}\n}$$</div>\n<p>A good idea executed badly can still become a bad investment result.</p></div></details></div></details>");
